  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nOur approach leverages a combination of multi-scale optimization with self-adaptive polynomial basis representations and dynamic constraint handling. We introduce a novel dimensionality-reduction technique based on orthogonal functional representations that automatically projects onto the feasible set while preserving the integral constraint. The core innovation is replacing the discrete grid representation with adaptive spectrum basis functions that capture shape features more efficiently. This reduces the effective search space while maintaining accuracy through tight integration bounds. The method addresses the high dimensionality and non-convexity issues by incorporating physics-inspired derivative information into the optimization landscape.\n\nKey features:\n- Adaptive polynomial-based basis functions for function representation\n- Polynomial-degree aware backtracking line search\n- Spectrum-adaptive integral normalization\n- Dynamic constraint enforcement through projected gradients\n- Multi-stage optimization from global to local extrema\n\nThe approach is motivated by both theoretical optimality and empirical results, addressing known weaknesses in the correlation metric handling and offering smoother trust-region transitions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- Discretization: Represent functions on [0,2] using orthogonal polynomial basis {\u03a6_k}_{k=0}^\u221e\n- Representation: f(x) = \u2211_{k=0}^{M} c_k \u03a6_k(x)\n- Constraints: \n  1. c_k \u2208 \u211d^{M\u00d7\u221e} projected onto feasible set\n  2. \u222b_0^2 f(x) dx = 1 (handled through \u03a6_0)\n- Objective: minimize max_k \u222b_0^2 f(x)(1-f(x+k)) dx\n- Search space: coefficients {c_k} with appropriate scaling\n- Regularization: c_k (\u03b1) = max(c_k, \u03b4) for stability\n- Polynomial scaling: \u03a6_k(x) = k(x)^m normal-normalized\n\nThe integral constraints are transformed into equivalent polynomial coefficients using multi-dimensional scaling, while non-negativity is enforced through Bezier-type clipping of the feature map.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nPhase 1: Self-similar parent extraction\n- Start with given parent if n=600, else use adaptive initialization\n- Extract multi-resolution basis {\u03a6_k} with adaptive kernel widths\n- Project parent onto polynomial subspace using adaptive thresholding\n- Perform dimension washing to optimize feature spectrum\n\nPhase 2: Optimization framework\n- Global phase (400s): Bayesian optimization with spectrum priorities\n- Local phase (300s): Quasi-Newton on adaptive basis\n- Refinement phase (200s): Progressive feature truncation\n\nCore operations:\n\ndef build_basis(n, max_degree=10):\n    \"\"\"Construct adaptive orthogonal basis\"\"\"\n    # Finite element style polynomial basis construction\n    # Includes endpoint handlers for non-normality\n    pass\n\ndef project_onto_feasible(c, n):\n    \"\"\"Produce valid integral and bounds\"\"\"\n    # Derivative-guided clipping\n    # Polynomial root-finding for clamping\n    pass\n\ndef spectrum_adaptive_gradient(c, n, alpha=1.0):\n    \"\"\"Compute optimized gradient\"\"\"\n    # Finite differences with polynomial interference\n    pass\n\nPhase 3: Handling resolution changes\n- From coarse grid:\n  * Downsample via sinc interpolation\n  * Adjust basis spectrum dynamically\n- From fine grid:\n  * Truncate least significant modes\n  * Maintain integral using auto-normalization\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\nUseful configurations:\n- Primary: \n  * Basis type: orthogonal polynomials (Legendre basis)\n  * Degree progression: 0->10->30 (with truncation)\n  * Spectrum weights: user-defined\n- Advanced variations:\n  * Fourier basis\n  * B-splines basis\n\nAdaptive rules:\n- Degree increase: based on curvature detection\n- Coefficients adaptation: dead-zone (magnitude <1e-7) flipping\n- Orthogonalization: restart with Gram-Schmidt when deviation >1%\n\n5. Compute budget:\nDominant cost: O(d^3) per basis function update\nEstimate:\n- 2000s for 600 points\n- 1000s for 300 points\n\nBreakdown:\n- Global phase: 400s (\u22481000 evals)\n- Local phase: 300s (\u2248500 evals)\n- Refinement: 200s (\u2248300 evals)\n\n6. Validation and contract:\n- Verify positive entries [0,1]\n- Confirm integral = nearest(n/2)\n- Check finite objective values\n- Return format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nFallback: Best of parent solutions or random init for n=600",
